## The idea

A pack that is too heavy makes a trek slower, more tiring and more dangerous. A common rule of thumb is to carry no more than about 20% of your body weight. This feature does that sum so the app can show people a sensible limit for their packing list.

## How it works

- The heaviest recommended pack is 20% of body weight, rounded down to the nearest half kilo. For example, 60 kg gives 12 kg, and 55 kg gives 11 kg.
- A second function gives a pack's weight as a percent of body weight. For example, a 10 kg pack on a 50 kg person is 20%.
- A third says whether a pack is too heavy. Exactly 20% is allowed; anything above is "too heavy".

Bad inputs, such as a zero or negative body weight, give a clear error.

## Where the code lives

- `packages/core/src/packWeight.ts`: the functions (pure maths, works offline).
- `packages/core/test/packWeight.test.ts`: automated tests that check the examples above.

## Limits

This is a tested building block. It does not appear on a screen yet; the team can connect it later, for example next to the packing list. The 20% figure is a general rule of thumb, not medical advice. Fitness, terrain and the length of the trek all matter.

